# Block direct web access to files/folders that should never be served,
# both locally and once this is deployed to Vimexx shared hosting.

# Public pages are now PHP (see partials/ for the shared header/footer);
# make sure / resolves to index.php regardless of the host's own default.
DirectoryIndex index.php index.html

<FilesMatch "^\.env">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

<FilesMatch "^(composer\.(json|lock)|phinx\.php)$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

RewriteEngine On
# "storage" here is defense-in-depth, not the actual protection: contact
# request attachments are stored one directory ABOVE the project root by
# default (see src/Service/ContactAttachmentStorage.php), so there is no
# project-root storage/ folder to serve in the first place. This entry only
# guards against a future project-root-relative storage/ path.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} ^/(vendor|db|src|docker|storage)(/|$) [NC]
RewriteRule ^ - [F,L]

# XML sitemap: /sitemap.xml is generated by sitemap.php from the database on
# every request (see App\Service\Sitemap). Crawlers and robots.txt keep the
# conventional .xml URL — nothing outside this one line knows a PHP file is
# behind it. There used to be a hand-maintained static sitemap.xml here; it
# listed 5 URLs, contained nothing from the shop, and had no way of noticing
# a new product, a new collection or a newly published page.
#
# Placed above the /portfolio/ and /collecties/ rules purely for readability:
# "sitemap.xml" contains a dot, so it can never match either of those
# slug-charset patterns, nor the single-segment CMS-page rule at the bottom
# of this file.
RewriteRule ^sitemap\.xml$ sitemap.php [L]

# robots.txt: generated by robots.php for the same reason the sitemap is.
# The static file that used to sit here typed the production domain into its
# Sitemap: line, so a second deployment of this codebase advertised somebody
# else's sitemap; the URL now comes from APP_URL like every canonical tag
# does (see App\Service\Robots). The file itself is gone, so nothing can be
# served from disk if this rule is ever removed by accident — a missing
# robots.txt is a well-understood "crawl everything", not a wrong one.
RewriteRule ^robots\.txt$ robots.php [L]

# Portfolio project detail pages: /portfolio/project-slug -> portfolio-detail.php?slug=project-slug.
# One dedicated, reusable template (see portfolio-detail.php) — not a generic
# path router — so this pattern is deliberately narrow (slug charset only,
# one path segment) rather than a catch-all rewrite. See MAIN.MD.
RewriteRule ^portfolio/([a-z0-9-]+)/?$ portfolio-detail.php?slug=$1 [L,QSA]

# Shop collection pages: /collecties/collection-slug -> collectie.php?slug=collection-slug.
# Same shape and reasoning as the Portfolio rule above: one dedicated,
# reusable template (see collectie.php), not a generic path router, so the
# pattern stays deliberately narrow (slug charset only, exactly two path
# segments).
#
# A two-segment path never matches the generic single-segment CMS-page rule
# below, so a collection can neither shadow nor be shadowed by a CMS page.
# And because every collection URL is namespaced under /collecties/, a
# collection slug can never collide with a root-level application route —
# which is why collection slugs need no App\Service\ReservedRoutes check the
# way CMS page slugs do.
#
# Products are deliberately NOT nested under this path: a product always
# keeps its own canonical /product.php?id=… URL, even when it appears in
# several collections. See MAIN.MD.
RewriteRule ^collecties/([a-z0-9-]+)/?$ collectie.php?slug=$1 [L,QSA]

# Blog (App\Module\BlogModule, see BLOG.md). Five rules, same shape and
# reasoning as the two above: dedicated templates behind narrow patterns, not
# a generic path router. The whole module lives under one URL word, so a post
# slug can never collide with an application route the way a CMS page slug
# can — which is why blog slugs need no App\Service\ReservedRoutes check.
#
# Order matters only for readability: "feed.xml" contains a dot, so it can
# never match the [a-z0-9-] slug pattern, and the two archive rules are
# three-segment paths the two-segment post rule can never match.
#
# blog.php and blog-post.php are real root-level files, so the generic
# single-segment CMS-page rule below skips /blog on its own (.php -f), and
# App\Module\BlogModule additionally reserves the word at save time so an
# editor is told rather than left to discover the collision.
#
# With the Blog module switched off these URLs still reach PHP, and every one
# of these templates answers with the site's own 404 (App\Module\ModuleGuard)
# — indistinguishable from a URL that never existed.
RewriteRule ^blog/feed\.xml$ blog-feed.php [L]
RewriteRule ^blog/?$ blog.php [L,QSA]
RewriteRule ^blog/categorie/([a-z0-9-]+)/?$ blog.php?category=$1 [L,QSA]
RewriteRule ^blog/tag/([a-z0-9-]+)/?$ blog.php?tag=$1 [L,QSA]
RewriteRule ^blog/([a-z0-9-]+)/?$ blog-post.php?slug=$1 [L,QSA]

# Dynamic CMS pages: one reusable template (pagina.php, see
# App\Service\PageContent) driven entirely by the pages table + the page
# builder. Generic on purpose: creating and publishing a new page in the
# admin (admin/pages.php) makes /<its-slug> work immediately, with no new
# RewriteRule, no new PHP file, and no migration.
#
# The two RewriteConds are what keep this from ever swallowing a real
# application route instead of the catch-all being unsafe on its own:
#   - `%{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d`   — skip when the bare word is an existing
#     top-level directory (admin, api, assets, db, docker, docs, partials,
#     scripts, src, tests, vendor, ...), same set the block-rule above this
#     one already protects from being served directly.
#   - `%{REQUEST_FILENAME}.php !-f` — skip when <word>.php exists at the
#     project root (index, diensten, shop, product, cart, checkout, contact,
#     over-mij, cookiebeleid, herroeping, bestelling-status, pagina,
#     portfolio, portfolio-detail, phinx, ...). Since every one of
#     this project's real root-level pages already has a matching .php file,
#     this single generic check protects all of them at once, automatically,
#     without listing them here — new application routes stay protected with
#     zero changes to this file.
# Both checks are filesystem-based (self-maintaining as files/folders are
# added or removed) — see App\Service\ReservedRoutes for the one thing they
# can't cover (a name with no matching file/folder, e.g. "storage") plus the
# admin-facing validation error when a slug collides; that class is the
# actual enforcement point for rejecting a colliding slug at save time, this
# rule is only the public routing side of the same rule set. It is also why
# the six system pages (index, shop, diensten, portfolio, over-mij, contact)
# keep resolving to their own templates rather than to pagina.php: each has a
# matching <word>.php at the project root.
#
# portfolio/<slug> above matches first regardless (it's a two-segment path,
# the single-segment pattern below never matches a "/" in the middle), so a
# CMS page can never be created with slug "portfolio" stealing that route
# either way.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME}.php !-f
RewriteRule ^([a-z0-9-]+)/?$ pagina.php?slug=$1 [L,QSA]

# Everything Apache itself could not resolve ends here, and it is the only
# point in this application where such a request reaches PHP at all: there is
# no front controller, so a URL that matches none of the rules above (an
# underscore, two path segments, a .html extension, a capital letter) is
# refused by Apache before any of this project's code runs.
#
# 404.php uses that moment for two things, in this order: it asks the Redirect
# Manager whether this URL was moved (App\Service\Redirects\RedirectGate, see
# REDIRECTS.md) and otherwise renders the site's own "Pagina niet gevonden"
# document with a real 404 status, instead of Apache's stock error page.
#
# This can never shadow a working URL: Apache only reaches an ErrorDocument
# once it has established that nothing serves the request.
ErrorDocument 404 /404.php
